  {
    "storyId": "AMSD-2041",
    "verdict": "needs_review",
    "reviewNotes": "OpenSpec was effectively a no-op: acceptanceChanged=false, splitCount=0, and its notes merely acknowledge that ACs were empty without producing any elaboration, splits, or technical depth. This defeats the purpose of the sequential pipeline where OpenSpec should elaborate requirements before Speckit reviews them. Speckit then compensated by deriving all 14 ACs from the story description and technical file hints — but this means Speckit performed both elaboration and review roles, collapsing the intended separation of concerns. The 14 derived ACs are thematically sound (multi-market coverage, connection recovery, security boundary, accessibility, concurrent edits), but because they were invented from a description rather than refined from original acceptance criteria, they carry scope-drift risk and must be validated against actual product intent. Additionally, both agents report acceptanceChanged=false despite 14 new ACs being created — a data-integrity failure that could cause downstream automation to miss the delta. No story splits were proposed despite the feature spanning three markets (GO/UP/MX) with real-time preview, content-type-specific behavior, and multiple edge-case categories — this likely warrants decomposition into 2-3 child stories. Human review needed to: (1) confirm the 14 ACs match product intent, (2) correct the acceptanceChanged flags, (3) evaluate whether story splits are warranted, (4) determine if OpenSpec should be re-run with explicit instructions to produce output.",
